Here's what was created:

**`ErrorBoundary.tsx`** — the component, **`ErrorBoundary.example.tsx`** — usage demo.

### How it works

| Feature | Detail |
|---|---|
| Error catching | `getDerivedStateFromError` sets error state; `componentDidCatch` captures the component stack |
| Logging | `console.error` logs both the error and `componentStack`; optional `onError` prop forwards to your reporter |
| Retry | "Try again" button resets state, triggering a fresh render of `children` |
| Dev details | Component stack shown in a `<details>` element only in non-production builds |
| Custom fallback | Pass a `fallback` prop to override the default UI entirely |

### Usage

```tsx
// Wrap any subtree
<ErrorBoundary onError={(err, info) => reportToSentry(err, info)}>
  <MyWidget />
</ErrorBoundary>

// With a custom fallback
<ErrorBoundary fallback={<p>Widget unavailable</p>}>
  <MyWidget />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

Note: error boundaries must be class components — React does not support the equivalent lifecycle hooks (`componentDidCatch` / `getDerivedStateFromError`) in function components.
